- `state/enhanced-climate.md` — REST/command transport, enhanced_climate example,
  Lovelace card.
- `state/event-latency.md` — async call_service, memory mirror, write-behind.
- `state/ui-shell.md` — `/s/<id>/` namespaces, tab bar, debug page, web shell.
- `state/insight.md` — change context + `who_changed`, history aggregates,
  retention overrides, the reminders lib. COMPLETE.
- `state/code-review.md` — review rounds 1 (v2.8.9) and 2 (v3.2.2), both CLOSED.
- (sse-spec.md has no implementation and no state file.)

Released versions and what shipped in them: `CHANGELOG.md`. Do not mirror it
here.

## Key decisions (do not re-litigate without good reason)
- **Lua VM stays gopher-lua.** arnodel/golua (5.4/5.5) evaluated 2026-06-13 and
  rejected: v0.x single-maintainer, verbose CPS bindings across ~30 sites, stop
  semantics need SetContext, 5.3+ integers would split the number type through
  the JSON layer. Revisit only if golua hits 1.0 AND Lua 5.1 becomes a real
  problem for script authors.
- **Event batching stays 100 ms by default** (`runner.go` batchWindow). Unbatched
  bursts REALLY overflowed script channels and dropped events — that is why it
  exists. Latency-sensitive scripts opt out with `ha.immediate_events()`; do not
  make immediate the default. Tracker apply stays BEFORE Dispatch in main.go so
  handlers read fresh state.
- **SQLite `synchronous=NORMAL`, not FULL.** Every state_changed commits on the
  write handle before dispatch; FULL's fsync jitter lands straight on handler
  latency. The mirror re-seeds from HA, history is throwaway, timers rebuild —
  do NOT "fix durability" back to FULL.
- **SQLite DSN pragmas must use `_pragma=name(value)`** — modernc silently
  ignores mattn-style params. `TestOpenDBEnablesWAL` is the canary.
- **Purge cutoff is computed in Go as RFC3339 and bound as a parameter** — never
  `datetime('now',…)` in SQL; the format mismatch breaks the string comparison.
- **History is write-behind and must never drop silently**: one goroutine batches
  a 1024-cap queue into single transactions; a full queue blocks with a warn, a
  failed batch is retried once then dropped loudly. Corollary accepted: an entity
  whose history aged out entirely gets one baseline row per restart.
- **`call_service` stays synchronous by default.** `wait = false` sends in order
  and routes the rejection to `on_exception` off-loop; do not flip the default.
- **`ha.after` persistence is best-effort** — it survives a restart only if
  registered at load time; callback-registered timers are dropped and logged.
  Catch-up fires a missed timer at most once, never replays every interval.
- **Scripts register themselves in-script** (`ha.on_state_change` style). No
  external manifest, ever.
- **Web shell: one document, script pages in an iframe.** Never inject into or
  rewrite a script's HTML (auto-injection and an SPA splice were both rejected).
  Every URL the shell emits is relative — under ingress it cannot know its
  prefix — and the 308 for a missing trailing slash writes its own Location
  header because http.Redirect would absolutize it out of the ingress prefix.
  Since v4.3.0 the framed page is NOT a scroller: the shell sizes the frame to
  its document, forces `overflow:hidden` on it (Chromium never chains a scroll
  out of a frame, so one scrollable pixel in there eats the whole gesture) and
  scrolls `#view`. Script pages must therefore lay out as documents — no
  `100vh`, no `position:fixed`.
- **Debug introspection accessors never touch an `*lua.LState`** — cached fields
  and atomics only. `logbuf` WRAPS the text handler, never replaces it.
- **The Lovelace card is one vanilla file**: plain HTML + HA CSS theme variables,
  never HA's undocumented styling primitives (`ha-button`, `ha-switch`, …) which
  churn across releases. `<ha-card>`, `<ha-icon>` and the editor's entity pickers
  are the allowed exceptions. Card assets are Materialized to /config/www/ha-lua
  on boot, so a card fix reaches a user only after a new image AND a restart.
- **Add-on layout**: the HA config dir is mounted at `/config` via
  `homeassistant_config` + `path: /config` (like ESPHome) — NOT `addon_config`,
  which hides files in a private `/addon_configs/<slug>`. Options arrive as
  `/data/options.json` + `$SUPERVISOR_TOKEN`; run.sh passes no flags.
- **Two rooted filesystems**: scripts root (fs module, require, LoadAll) and logs
  root over log_dir. `ha.exceptions.log_file` takes relative paths only and
  raises at registration on absolute/`../`/unset log_dir.
- **Hot reload** debounces 300 ms then stats the file — present reloads, absent
  stops; never decide from fsnotify event types (atomic saves lie). Only `.lua`
  is watched: editing a sibling `.html` alone does NOT reload.
- **Core REST bindings are non-raising** (`value|nil, err`) so a per-minute
  publisher cannot spam `on_exception`. The REST base is derived from the WS URL,
  not configured.
- **`ha.at` timezone**: config option → `$TZ` → UTC-with-warning; `time/tzdata`
  embedded, next_run stored UTC, and main sets `time.Local` so script
  `time.now()` agrees with the scheduler.
- **json/v2 everywhere**, and deterministic key order is NOT its default — every
  marshal site that needs stable output passes `json.Deterministic(true)`.
- **One shared http client, fixed 30 s timeout**, plus the script's lifetime
  context. No per-callback context, no timeout config option.
- **Tests use real SQLite (`:memory:`), never mocks**; browser coverage is
  chromedp and skips cleanly when no browser is installed. Benchmark comparison
  is informational — it never fails the build.
- **Analyzers are `tool` directives in go.mod**, run as `go tool <name>`; errcheck
  exclusions are configured in golangci-lint rather than littering the code with
  `_ =`.
- **No vendoring** — go.sum plus the module proxy pin everything; `go mod vendor`
  is one command away if hermetic builds are ever needed.

## Removed (do not add back without explicit user request)
- state_stats_hourly / state_stats_daily tables, `ha.get_stats`, the `is_numeric`
  column, and the 4-step transactional purge
- `global.state()` persistent proxy (cache coherency across concurrent scripts)
- `cmd/bench-check` (over-engineered for a home project)
- the `states` mirror table (memory-authoritative since v3.1.0)
- the battery example's static `IGNORE` table (replaced by the page's toggle)

## User preferences observed
- Pure Go only — modernc.org/sqlite and gopher-lua, no CGO.
- Plan before writing code.
- **ASK WHAT THE FAILURE LOOKS LIKE before theorising** about a UI bug. The
  v4.3.0–v4.3.2 scroll bug took three attempts because the first two were
  inferred from symptoms; none of it ever reproduced in headless Chromium at any
  DPR, so on device-visible bugs the device is the only verdict.
- Keep this file lean: if the code, CLAUDE.md, CHANGELOG.md or git says it,
  it does not belong here.
